      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"30\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mechanism Check</text><rect x=\"170\" y=\"200\" width=\"760\" height=\"200\" rx=\"16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CO2 tolerance rises with training,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"295\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">but oxygen can still fall dangerously low</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"350\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Warning signal is blunted, not oxygen supply improved</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"500\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Not lung shrinkage, not zero effect,</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"535\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">and it affects experienced divers too</text></svg>",
      "caption": "The correct explanation is that CO2 tolerance training blunts the breathing urge signal while oxygen levels can still drop dangerously, a risk that affects experienced divers as well."
